{{also|Tinker}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|tinkere}}, perhaps from {{inh|en|ang|*tincere}}, from {{m|ang|tin|t=tin}} + {{der|en|ang|*cere}} (as in {{m|ang|bēocere|t=beekeeper}}), from {{der|en|gem-pro|*kazjaz|t=vessel-maker}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*kazą|t=vessel; vat; tub}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/tɪŋkə(ɹ)/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/tɪŋkɚ/}}
* {{a|US}} {{audio|en|en-us-tinker.ogg}}
* {{hyphenation|en|tin|ker}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋkə(r)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An [[itinerant]] tinsmith and [[mender]] of [[household]] [[utensil]]s made of [[metal]].
# {{lb|en|dated|chiefly|British|and|Irish|offensive}} A member of the [[Irish Traveller]] community. A [[gypsy]].
# {{lb|en|usually with "little"}} A [[mischievous]] person, especially a playful, [[impish]] youngster.
# Someone who repairs, or attempts repair, on anything mechanical, or who invents such devices; one who [[#Verb|tinker]]s; a [[tinkerer]].
# The act of repair or invention. {{rfex|en}}
# {{lb|en|military|obsolete}} A hand [[mortar]].
# Any of various fish: the {{vern|chub mackerel|lang=en}}, the [[silverside]], the [[skate]], or a young [[mackerel]] about two years old.
# A bird, the razor-billed [[auk]].

